# Temporal Activities - Complete Implementation Guide

**

## What is an Activity?

An **Activity** is a unit of work that represents a single, well-defined action in your workflow. Activities contain the actual business logic that interacts with external systems.

### Key Characteristics

✅ **Non-Deterministic**: Can make external calls, generate random values
✅ **Retriable**: Automatically retried on failure
✅ **Timeout-Based**: Multiple timeout controls
✅ **Heartbeat Support**: Long-running activities can report progress
✅ **Idempotent**: Should be safe to retry
✅ **Cancellable**: Can be gracefully cancelled

## Activity vs Workflow

| Aspect | Workflow | Activity |
|--------|----------|----------|
| Purpose | Orchestration | Execution |
| Determinism | Must be deterministic | Can be non-deterministic |
| External calls | ❌ Not allowed | ✅ Allowed |
| Retries | Workflow-level | Built-in activity retries |
| Duration | Minutes to years | Seconds to hours |
| State | Durable | Stateless |

## Activity Implementation Patterns

### Pattern 1: Simple Activity

**Use Case**: Basic operation with external interaction

```typescript
// TypeScript Example
export async function sendEmail(to: string, subject: string, body: string): Promise<void> {
  const transporter = nodemailer.createTransport({
    host: process.env.SMTP_HOST,
    port: 587,
  });
  
  await transporter.sendMail({
    from: 'noreply@example.com',
    to,
    subject,
    text: body,
  });
  
  console.log(`Email sent to ${to}`);
}
```

```python
